-1.2 On any bounded time interval choose a Lipschitz constant $C$ for $F$ on a ball containing the solution. Set $n_i^+=\max(n_i,0)$ and $u(t)=\sum_i\max(-n_i(t),0)$. For each coordinate, step 1.1 and Lipschitz continuity give $F_i(n,L)\ge F_i(n^+,L)-C\|n-n^+\|_2\ge-Cu$. The absolutely continuous negative-part chain rule gives $(\max(-n_i,0))'=-F_i$ when $n_i<0$, zero when $n_i>0$, and zero almost everywhere on the level set $n_i=0$. Hence $u'(t)\le m C u(t)$ almost everywhere. Since $u(0)=0$ and $u\ge0$, differentiating $e^{-mCt}u(t)$ shows it is nonincreasing from zero, so $u(t)=0$. Once $n\ge0$, the reservoir derivative is a sum of nonnegative overflow rates, hence $L(t)\ge0$. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
